I was finally home I had just moved back  from Johannesburg South Africa where I  had been living for two years I was  Thanksgiving was a few days away and I  was sitting it on the couch with my dad  watching the news coming out of Ferguson  Michael Brown was dead and they had just  decided not to indict Darren Wilson not  even give him a trial what my dad looked  at me and he said what did you expect to  happen I mean if it was dark and a black  guy came at me with something in his  hand and a hood on I mean I could have  done the same thing  I stood up shaking and I looked at my  dad and before I ran out of the room I  said you are a racist I didn't  understand how my dad could have said  that his dad was a Dixiecrat defense  lawyer like Atticus Finch and to kill a  mockingbird  he'd spent his whole life fighting for  justice and equality that was the reason  why I had gone on to study racism as an  American Studies major in college at the  University of Virginia but the racism  that I studied was an extreme slave  owners were racist Ku Klux Klan members  were racist separate-but-equal racist  but me my family we were good so how  would I just called my dad a racist  conflicted I needed to figure it out  and when I learned is that racism is not  black and white  it is not binary instead it is a  spectrum and we are all wired to be on  it but when we talk about racism we put  up walls so to take those walls down  today we're gonna coin a new term we're  gonna call it Oh didi other deficit  disorder  with a deficit of the other in our lives  we have a disorder that causes us to  unintentionally avoid discriminate and  harm this is how we're wired we get OD D  from two sources direct experience and  indirect exposure to stereotypes when I  reflected on my OD D I realized that I  started when I was five now I was about  to start school and I was still sucking  my thumb and my mom who's here in the  audience is probably still embarrassed  she was horrified so she went she  consulted her brother-in-law my uncle  Robert and he had a solution you see  uncle Robert was Chinese and he had a  secret potion that he painted on both of  my thumbs that made them taste like  rotting Brussels sprouts from that  moment I needed uncle Robert and from my  limited five-year-old perspective I  didn't know any other Chinese people so  I thought that all Chinese people were  bad and should be avoided and it sounds  silly but 20 years later I looked around  I still didn't have any clothes Chinese  friends this is how we're wired this is  made even worse every day by the  indirect stereotypes that we absorb in  American media for example black men  play violent roles 56 percent of the  time while white men play them only 11  percent Asian men on the other hand are  often portrayed as effeminate nerds but  why it goes back to 1850 San Francisco  when 4000 Chinese immigrants flooded  into the city and only two were women  white men felt threatened by these new  eligible bachelors and so they used  political cartoons to brand them as a  feminine making fun of them for doing  girly work even though those were the  only jobs available as a result the  American public came to expect Asian men  who might be good at math but not at  wooing women all because white men  150 years ago were afraid of losing  their ladies this is how were wired  I mentioned that I studied racism in  college but while I spent afternoons in  the library getting all fired up about  the injustice of separate but equal I  came home to a different kind I was in a  sorority in college was actually  president of my sorority in college and  I know a lot of you are already bringing  negative stereotypes in to make  judgements about me but we won't talk  about that today but aside from all of  the negatives there are a lot of  positives too and one of the positives  is that you get to live with all of your  best friends all the time but when I  think back to that house of 146 people  there is only one person of color Lamont  our cook  we hadn't tried to keep the other out  but we had tried to bring people like us  in the result is discrimination and you  don't lose that bias when you're looking  for friends looking to hire someone or  looking for a life partner this is how  we're wired even more dangerous than  discrimination though is unintentional  harm Stanford psychologist Jennifer  Eberhardt studies implicit racial bias  and its impact on our behavior in one  study she showed participants images of  either black men or white men and then  uh fuzzy images of objects that slowly  became clear as a result when the  objects were crime-related and  participants had been shown images of  black men they were able to identify  them that much faster  Eber Hart's work suggests that we are  culturally conditioned to associate  black men with crime so my dad may have  been right about the impact that culture  had had on him we are all wired with OD  D and asking yourself where am I on this  spectrum is a really hard question to  ask because the answer often hurts when  I talk about this I feel sad and I feel  a tremendous amount of shame but when  I'm able to separate myself Who I am  from the mistakes I've made and the  mistakes that were made before me I feel  empowered and the most important message  I have for you today is that no matter  how we're wired on this spectrum we have  an opportunity to do some rewiring the  turning point for me  came when I moved to South Africa and  when you start talking about it it's  okay to say I'm white you're black it's  not just okay it's necessary because  when we tiptoe around the topics the way  we do here we give them power by saying  nothing the only thing you're doing is  is making sure that nothing changes what  if instead we stayed open we're curious  ask questions and if we're uncomfortable  as we will be it's okay  today we're uncomfortable and then ask  anyway because once we start the  conversation then we can actually start  to build the relationships that rewire  racism when I arrived an African  Leadership Academy to teach in  Johannesburg I had created a situation  accidentally in which I have become the  other so much so that my students  nicknamed me Leblanc as if I couldn't  crack that code but after a few weeks at  a la African other became Nigerian  Rwandan Senegalese Kenyan individuals  and when you can see diversity within an  other racial group it becomes really  hard to hold on to the stereotypes that  you came in with that African other  became some of my closest friends who I  still talk to daily because when you're  able to find common ground that's deeper  than the color of your skin you're able  to make connections that last longer -  this is a process this is a hard process  this is a long process this is something  I'm still working on I'm still working  on rewiring the OTD that I developed as  a child by building a relationship with  my roommate here at Stanford who is full  of joy a fantastic cook looks awesome in  hats and his Chinese she just happens to  be a little bit nicer than uncle Robert  was to me  I'm still working on this  we all have Oh didi but when we stop  accepting what others have shaped for us  and start shaping it ourselves then we  can rewire racism thank you
 

